################################################################
# WHY THERE IS NO INVERTED CONTROL HERE
################################################################

The usual negative control does not exist for this task, and manufacturing
something shaped like one would be worse than saying so plainly.

A negative control works by suspending the fix and watching the test go red:
it proves the test is sensitive to the thing the task changed. T-111 removed
a feature. There is no fix to suspend. "Inverting" it would mean restoring
five main classes, one test class, a DTO, two payment-port methods and a
database table — and then watching a 404 assertion stop being a 404. That
demonstrates only that deleted code is deleted, which nobody doubted, and it
would cost an hour of somebody's reading to establish nothing.

What a removal actually has to prove is a different pair of statements, and
both are made below, in ONE test class and ONE Spring context so that neither
can be true of a different build from the other:

  1. THE SURFACE IS GONE FROM THE RUNNING APPLICATION.  Not merely uncalled,
     not merely unreachable from a screen — absent from the request mapping.
     All four recurring endpoints are asked for while SIGNED IN (they were
     @PreAuthorize("isAuthenticated()"), so a 403 would be an authorisation
     answer and would leave the mapping's existence an open question) and all
     four answer 404. The schema is asked the same question directly:
     recurring_plans does not exist and donations.recurring_plan_id does not
     exist, read out of information_schema rather than trusted to the
     migration file.

     V114's DROP TABLE is deliberately written WITHOUT CASCADE. That this
     context booted at all is therefore the runtime check that no foreign key
     besides donations_recurring_plan_fk pointed into recurring_plans: an
     unknown dependant would have failed Flyway here, loudly, instead of being
     quietly taken down with the table.

  2. WHAT STAYED IS UNDAMAGED.  The whole of OneTimeDonationIT runs in the
     same file — checkout creating a PENDING record, a signed webhook and only
     a signed webhook completing it, idempotency on a duplicate, the expiry
     sweep, the rescue of a gift that was really paid for, and reconciliation.
     The control test finishes by driving a full one-time donation end to end
     itself. Ten pre-existing tests plus the new one, all green.

  And one thing the removal deliberately did NOT do is asserted too, so that a
  later tidying pass meets a failing test rather than a silent decision:
  'RECURRING' remains a legal value of donations.type. A donation row is the
  temple's record of money it actually received, and narrowing that CHECK
  would mean rewriting or destroying one.

